var PARAS = {
easy1:"The bell of the government school rings at eight in the morning, and children come running from every lane of the small town. Most of them walk in small groups with their bags on their backs and lunch boxes in their hands. A few older boys and girls come on bicycles, while the youngest children are brought by their parents. The teachers reach the school before the bell and open the doors of every classroom. The day begins with a short prayer and the national anthem in the open ground. After the prayer, the children move to their classes in neat and quiet lines. The class teacher first marks the attendance of all students in a big register. Then the lessons of the day begin with reading, writing and simple sums. The children study Hindi, English, mathematics, science and social studies with interest. The teacher writes the main points on the blackboard and asks easy questions. Bright students help the slow learners, so nobody feels left behind in the class. At noon, all the children sit together in rows and eat the hot meal served in the school. This midday meal gives them energy and keeps them active during the afternoon classes. Once a week, there are periods for drawing, music, games and moral stories. The school also has a small library with story books, poems and picture books. Every Friday, one full period is kept for silent reading in the library. The head teacher visits each class, checks the notebooks and guides the young teachers. Parents are called to the school once a month to discuss the progress of their children. Clean drinking water, tidy classrooms and separate toilets keep the school safe and healthy. When the last bell rings at three, the children leave in groups, still talking about the day. A good school is the pride of any village or town. It gives every child, rich or poor, a fair chance to learn and grow in life.",
easy2:"Good health is the real wealth of a person, and it begins with simple daily habits. We should wake up early, take a bath and eat a clean and balanced breakfast. Fresh fruits, green vegetables, milk and pulses keep the body strong and active. Junk food and cold drinks may taste good, but they harm the body in the long run. Every village and town has a primary health centre where treatment is given free of cost. A doctor, a nurse and a few health workers serve the people at this centre. Patients reach the centre in the morning and give their names at the registration counter. The nurse notes the name, age and problem of each patient in a register. The doctor then examines the patients one by one and writes the proper medicines. Simple tests of blood and sugar are also done at the centre on fixed days. Medicines are given from the store of the centre, and their use is explained clearly to every patient. Mothers bring small babies for vaccination on the immunisation day every week. These vaccines protect children from many serious diseases in early life. The health workers visit homes and teach families about clean water and sanitation. They tell people to keep their houses and drains clean and to cover the stored water. During the rainy season, they warn people about malaria and other seasonal diseases. If a patient is serious, the centre sends him to the district hospital by ambulance. The ambulance service is free and can be called at any hour of the day or night. Old people are given special care, and their blood pressure is checked at regular intervals. Health camps are also held in villages, where free check-ups are done for all. A healthy family spends less on medicines and lives a happier and more peaceful life. That is why every person should take proper care of his health from a young age itself.",
med1:"Public administration is the machinery through which the policies of the government reach the common citizen. At the district level, this machinery is headed by the District Magistrate, who is responsible for law and order, revenue work and the general supervision of all departments. Below the district headquarters, the tehsil office deals with land records, caste and income certificates, and the day-to-day problems of farmers and residents. A citizen who needs a certificate today does not have to visit the office again and again, because most services are now available through online portals and common service centres. The application is received, entered in a register or a computer system, and given a diary number by which its progress can be tracked at any time. The dealing clerk examines the papers, the section officer records his remarks, and the competent authority finally approves or rejects the request with reasons recorded on the file. Time limits have been fixed for the disposal of most services, and a delay without a valid reason invites an explanation from the officer concerned. Public grievances form another important part of the work of district administration. Complaints received during public hearing days are marked to the concerned departments, and the action taken reports are reviewed every week at the district level. Pension cases of retired employees, widow pension, old age pension and disability assistance are processed by the social welfare section of the district. Field officers such as the revenue inspector and the lekhpal verify the facts on the spot before any benefit is sanctioned to an applicant. During elections, the same administrative machinery prepares the voter lists, trains the polling staff and ensures that voting takes place peacefully. In times of flood, drought or any other emergency, the district administration becomes the first responder for relief and rescue operations. Coordination between different departments is achieved through regular meetings, written orders and a proper system of reporting at every level. Records of all these decisions are preserved carefully in files, because the noting of today becomes the evidence of tomorrow. A district office that works with honesty, speed and courtesy earns the lasting trust of the public it serves. That trust, in the end, is the true measure of good administration in a democracy.",
med2:"The police station is the most visible face of the government for an ordinary citizen. From early morning till late night, its doors remain open for anyone who needs help, protection or information of any kind. The work of the station begins with the daily diary, in which every report, order and movement of staff is recorded in proper order. When a citizen comes with a complaint, the duty officer listens patiently and reduces the information into writing. If the complaint shows a cognisable offence, a first information report is registered, and a copy of it is given to the complainant free of cost. The investigating officer then visits the spot, records the statements of witnesses and collects whatever evidence is available at the scene. Beat constables are allotted fixed areas of the town, which they patrol on foot or by motorcycle during the day and the night. This regular patrolling creates a sense of security among shopkeepers, women, students and senior citizens of the locality. Traffic police manage the busy crossings, challan the vehicles that break the rules, and keep the roads clear for ambulances and fire tenders. A separate women help desk receives complaints from women and children and deals with them with extra care and complete privacy. Senior officers inspect the station at regular intervals and check the registers, the lock-up, the weapons and the pending investigation files. Community policing meetings are held with residents, traders and school authorities to discuss local problems before they grow serious. During festivals, elections and public processions, additional force is deployed, and the routes are planned in advance in consultation with the organisers. Modern stations also maintain a facility where complaints of online fraud and cheating are received and forwarded to the cyber cell for action. The station house officer remains responsible for the discipline of the staff and for the quality of service delivered to the public. People judge the entire police force by the behaviour of the single constable whom they meet at the gate or on the road. Courtesy, honesty and quick response are therefore not extra qualities but the basic duty of every member of the force.",
hard1:"The district judiciary occupies a central position in the legal system of the country, for it is at this level that the overwhelming majority of citizens come into direct contact with the administration of justice. Every district court complex houses the courts of the District and Sessions Judge, the Chief Judicial Magistrate, and a number of civil judges and judicial magistrates, each exercising the jurisdiction defined by statute. Litigation commences, in a civil matter, with the presentation of a plaint; in a criminal matter, with the filing of a charge sheet (challan) by the police, or a complaint by the aggrieved party. Upon institution, the case is entered in the register, a number is allotted, and summonses are issued to the opposite party through the process serving agency of the court. The figures themselves reveal the magnitude of the challenge: according to official data published in 2024, more than 4.4 crore cases were pending in the district and subordinate courts of India, of which approximately 71 per cent were criminal in nature. It is frequently observed that \"justice delayed is justice denied\"; yet the maxim must be balanced against the equally compelling principle that haste, in adjudication, is the surest enemy of accuracy. To address the backlog, the e-Courts project has computerised over 18,000 courts, and case information, cause lists and certified copies of orders are now accessible to litigants through a public portal. Lok Adalats, organised at regular intervals under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, disposed of more than 1.1 crore cases in a single year, thereby demonstrating the efficacy of consensual settlement. A decree passed by a civil court attains finality unless an appeal is preferred within the period of limitation prescribed by law (ordinarily 30 days for appeals within the district, and 90 days to the High Court). The ministerial staff of the court, comprising readers, stenographers, clerks and copyists, bears a responsibility that is seldom acknowledged; an error in a date, a name, or a section of law may occasion an adjournment, multiply the cost of litigation, and erode public confidence in the institution. Witnesses are examined and cross-examined in open court; documents are exhibited, marked and proved in accordance with the law of evidence; and the judgment, when pronounced, must record reasons for every conclusion arrived at. Legal aid clinics functioning within the court premises provide free assistance to persons whose annual income falls below the ceiling fixed by the State Authority (presently Rs. 3,00,000 in several States). It has been rightly remarked by a former Chief Justice that \"the court is not a building of brick and mortar; it is the faith of the common man that his grievance will be heard.\" So long as that faith endures, and so long as every functionary of the system, from the presiding officer to the process server, discharges his duty with diligence and impartiality, the district court will continue to remain the firmest pillar of the rule of law in the Republic.",
hard2:"Disaster management, as defined under the Disaster Management Act, 2005, is a continuous and integrated process of planning, organising, coordinating and implementing measures necessary for the prevention of danger, the mitigation of risk, and preparedness for dealing with any threatening situation. India, owing to its unique geo-climatic conditions, is among the most disaster-prone countries of the world; nearly 59 per cent of its landmass is susceptible to earthquakes of varying intensity, while about 12 per cent is prone to floods and river erosion. The institutional framework functions at three levels, namely the National Disaster Management Authority, the State Authorities in every State, and the District Authorities chaired by the District Magistrate, and each level is required to prepare, and periodically revise, its own disaster management plan. Early warning constitutes the first line of defence: the India Meteorological Department issues cyclone and heavy rainfall alerts 72 hours in advance, and the Central Water Commission monitors river levels at 331 flood forecasting stations across the country. When the cyclone \"Remal\" made landfall in May 2024, more than 7 lakh persons were evacuated to relief shelters in the affected coastal districts; the casualty figure, consequently, remained far lower than in comparable events of earlier decades. The National Disaster Response Force, raised in 2006, presently comprises 16 battalions (approximately 18,000 personnel) deployed at 28 locations, so positioned that a team can reach any disaster site within the shortest possible time. Rescue operations, however, are only the visible part of the response; the less visible, and often more demanding, part consists of restoring drinking water, electricity, road connectivity and medical services in the affected area. Relief is distributed in accordance with the norms of the State Disaster Response Fund, under which assistance of Rs. 4,00,000 is admissible to the family of a deceased person, and graded assistance is provided for damaged houses, crops and livestock. Mock drills are conducted in schools, hospitals and government offices at least twice a year, for it is an established finding of disaster research that communities which have rehearsed their response suffer measurably fewer casualties. The role of the ordinary citizen must not be underestimated; a resident who observes the warning, secures loose objects, stores drinking water and assists a neighbour contributes as materially to the outcome as any uniformed agency. As the Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction (2015-2030) observes, the objective is not merely to respond to disasters but to \"build back better\" in recovery, rehabilitation and reconstruction. Training of community volunteers, preparation of village-level contingency plans, and pre-positioning of essential supplies in vulnerable blocks are inexpensive measures whose value is realised precisely on the day when everything else fails. It follows, therefore, that disaster management is not the concern of a single department but a collective discipline, in which science, administration and community participation must advance together if losses of life and property are to be progressively reduced."
};

var SIMPASS = "The Indian Railways is not merely a mode of transport; it is one of the largest employers, one of the oldest institutions, and, for millions of citizens, the most familiar face of the Indian state. The first train in the country ran on 16 April 1853, covering a distance of 34 kilometres between Bori Bunder (Mumbai) and Thane, and was hauled by three locomotives named Sahib, Sultan and Sindh. From that modest beginning, the network has expanded to over 68,000 route kilometres and more than 7,300 stations, making it the fourth largest railway system in the world under a single management. On an average day, the Railways carry approximately 2.4 crore passengers, a number comparable to the entire population of Australia, besides loading nearly 4.5 million tonnes of freight. The freight basket itself reflects the economy it serves: coal accounts for roughly 50 per cent of the loading, followed by iron ore, cement, foodgrains, fertilisers and petroleum products. Administration of this vast system is exercised through 19 zones, each headed by a General Manager, and 68 operating divisions, which are responsible for train operations, maintenance of track, and the safety of passengers within their jurisdiction. Safety has, in recent decades, received unprecedented attention; consequential train accidents, which numbered 473 in the year 2000-01, declined to fewer than 50 in 2023-24, a reduction attributed to track renewal, elimination of unmanned level crossings, and the progressive fitment of the indigenous automatic train protection system, Kavach. The financial structure of the Railways presents its own complexities: the operating ratio, namely the amount spent to earn every 100 rupees, stood at approximately 98.4 per cent in the revised estimates for 2023-24, leaving only a narrow margin for capital investment from internal resources. Modernisation is nevertheless visible at every level; the Vande Bharat trainsets, manufactured indigenously, now operate on more than 100 routes, while station redevelopment under the Amrit Bharat scheme covers 1,300 stations in its first phase. For the passenger, the transformation is most evident in the booking hall that has migrated to the telephone: over 80 per cent of reserved tickets are now booked electronically, and refunds, chart status and train running information are available in real time. Railway employees, numbering about 12 lakh, work in shifts that recognise neither festival nor weather; the gangman who inspects the track at dawn and the guard who signs off the last goods train at midnight are as much a part of the system as the officers who direct it. It has often been said that \"if the Railways stop, India stops\"; the statement is an exaggeration only in form, for in substance the movement of coal to the power plant, of grain to the ration shop, and of the soldier to his post depends upon these rails. Season tickets, unreserved general coaches and the suburban services of the great metropolitan cities together account for the bulk of this traffic, and it is in these crowded compartments, rather than in the air-conditioned coaches, that the everyday India actually travels. Freight corridors dedicated exclusively to goods trains are now being commissioned in phases, and their completion is expected to release valuable capacity on the existing network for faster and more punctual passenger services. As the network moves towards its target of complete electrification of broad gauge routes and a freight share of 45 per cent by 2030, the institution that began with a 34 kilometre experiment in 1853 continues, quietly and indispensably, to bind the geography of the Republic into a single economic whole.";

